        },
        "markdown": "# 11 · Juror Test and Final Evidence Recap\n## 1:16:46–1:37:26\n\nLionel argues that continued public attacks only introduce more people to the disputed claims. He returns to the trial-lawyer frame and asks the audience to imagine serving as jurors evaluating two alleged vectors of guilt: the weapon and the confession. \n\nHe revives the lint-brush and bazooka analogies to dramatize his assertion that an object not linked to the wound proves nothing, then repeatedly asks how a juror could connect Tyler Robinson to the shooting. \n\nThe section expands into questions about witnesses, the surviving defendant, public advocates, and why so many people resist conspiracy framing. \n\n**Function:** close the analytical loop by returning from media criticism to the proof standard announced near the beginning.",
        "memo": "",
        "link": "",
        "visibilityRole": "browser",
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "create",
          "updatedAt": 1786055142666,
